untangling our membership from the eu is  a form of high-stakes  diplomatic gymnastics fraught with risk  hence the backstop for northern ireland  which some see as safety net and others  as a way of tying us up in knots  the back stops a bit like a venn diagram  so when england scotland wales and  northern ireland are within the eu  they have the same regulations external  tariffs with no internal tariffs  but if they pull apart there's a  conundrum to allow frictionless movement  to the republic of ireland  from northern ireland they'd need to  maintain regulations and tariffs from  the eu  but that would create a border in the  irish sea with the rest of the uk  but if northern ireland pulls away with  the uk  then it creates a land border with  ireland so the process of separation  appears to require you to choose between  the two options  the backstop blurs this it keeps the  whole of the uk in a customs union  with the eu while northern ireland is  more closely linked and also remains in  the single market  some argue this would be an indefinite  bind  locks us in we have no vote to leave the  eu will not negotiate with us fairly  they will force us towards it and they  will get the concessions and we will be  in a very difficult position and worst  of all  the dup simply can't accept it so we  don't have a government  legal text from the attorney general  advised the backstop could endure  indefinitely  but easy movement across this border is  particularly sensitive because it is  fundamental to the good friday agreement  that ended the troubles in northern  ireland the irish and the eu side have  always maintained that the backstop in  its current form is the only way to  protect the spirit of the good friday  agreement  by keeping the irish border open no  matter what which was  essentially the foundation on which the  good friday agreement was built  so what can theresa may do i will be  seeking legal and political assurances  she could ask for a form of binding  legal text  to sit alongside the backstop agreement  it could either commit the eu to  negotiating a free trade arrangement  within a specified time  or have a simple time limit to the  backstory but the eu could argue  then it is no longer a backstop the eu  can say  that he hopes this backstop is  short-term and not forever  but it's actually like to be rather  long-term because so far nobody's  thought of a way of  avoiding the need for controls on that  uh  border between the northern ireland and  the south of ireland except with the  backstop  but unless theresa may can get a real  concession on the backstop she's going  to require major acrobatics to get her  deal  through parliament jason farrell sky  news
 

